{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the eighth day.</b> This is the eighth day of the ordination. It would appear from this that this day too is included among the days of ordination that preceded it — but that is not so, for Scripture says (Leviticus 8:33), ״כִּי שִׁבְעַת יָמִים יְמַלֵּא אֶת יֶדְכֶם״. And even though this day was an ordination for the dedication of the altar, none the less — what does the dedication of Aaron and his sons have to do with the dedication of the altar?\nAnd perhaps it is because He wanted to say that on that very day HaShem appeared to them and not on the preceding days — therefore He needed to give a reason: what day is this among days? Because this day was the eighth, that is what brought it a sanctity of surpassing exaltation, for every count of seven is profane and the count of eight is holy, according to the view of the Midrash (Yalkut Shim'oni, Exodus 201:15), which says that every praise of Moses was with 'אָז' — ״וּמֵאָז בָאתִי לְדַבֵּר בִּשְׁמֶךָ״, ״אָז יָשִׁיר מֹשֶׁה״, etc. — for 'אָז' means one riding upon seven, and that is to grant dominion to HaShem, blessed be He, over all seven planetary stars and over all things that came into being during the seven days of Creation. And therefore HaShem appeared to them specifically on this day, by virtue of its being the eighth, for this number is uniquely His, blessed be He. And this is the reason that an offering is not accepted except from the eighth day onward; and this is the reason that circumcision on the eighth day overrides the Sabbath that falls on the seventh — for the spiritual overrides the material." "And the fact that this eighth day took ten crowns alludes to what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 13b): the harp of the days of the Messiah will have eight strings, and that of the world to come ten strings — because in the days of the Messiah, when the glory of HaShem is revealed and all flesh will see that HaShem is one, He rides upon the seven planetary stars that guide this world; but in the world to come, when they will be entirely stripped of matter, there will be added in them a grasp such that they will recognize the power of His kingdom, blessed be He, over all the supernal separate intelligences, which are included in the count of nine — in the mystical secret of 'the Ark nine and the Covering a handbreadth' — and then the eighth will ascend to the count of ten. Therefore the allusion comes through this eighth day, which took ten crowns, to say that it contains something resembling the world to come, for there they will see the glory of HaShem eye to eye; and so too on this day it is said, ״כִּי הַיּוֹם ה׳ נִרְאָה אֲלֵיכֶם״." "<b>And to the elders of Israel.</b> We do not find that He spoke to all the elders. And if it is as the commentators say — that He spoke to Aaron in the presence of the elders of Israel so that they should not say Aaron entered the service on his own initiative — behold it was already said in the portion of Tzav (8:3), ״וְאֵת כָּל הָעֵדָה הַקְהֵל״, etc., and there He installed him into the service in the presence of the entire assembly; so why was this needed here a second time?\nAnd it appears to me that since it is written (Leviticus 4:13–15), ״וְאִם כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁגּוּ, וְסָמְכוּ זִקְנֵי הָעֵדָה יְדֵיהֶם עַל רֹאשׁ הַפָּר״ — and likewise in these communal offerings that came to atone for ״וַיִּשְׁחֲטוּ שְׂעִיר עִזִּים״ (Genesis 37:31) and for the matter of the calf, presumably they did with it everything specified in the Torah as expounded in the portion of VaYikra concerning the sin-offering of the congregation. Therefore He called the elders. And even though it is not mentioned here that they leaned their hands, none the less presumably they performed the leaning. And even though every sin-offering of the congregation — its offering is a bull — here it is different, for they sinned with a goat and a calf; therefore HaShem wanted them to bring a goat and a calf."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The verse quotations ״ומאז באתי״ and ״אז ישיר משה״ are from Exodus 5:23 and Exodus 15:1 respectively; the citation reference after each is editorial and not in the source.", 1 "The phrase 'Ark nine and the Covering a handbreadth' (ארון תשעה וכפרת טפח) refers to a talmudic measurement (Sukkah 5a / Bava Batra 99a) treated here as a mystical number-scheme; rendered literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:12.585701343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}